---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The comprehensive ClassicBars reference — the Handlebars-faithful surface. Modelled
// on the handlebarsjs.com guide's spine (expressions → paths → block helpers →
// built-ins → subexpressions → custom helpers → partials → comments) but NOT a
// copy: it leads with what works exactly like Handlebars, then a named "Where
// ClassicBars diverges" section sourced from classicbars-compat.adoc. Prose is
// illustrative only; every runnable example comes from the single gate-validated
// source ../classicbars.mjs and renders through the real `surface` engine. The
// ~80-helper prelude is NOT re-listed here (that is the generated catalog's job) —
// this page teaches the language and the six built-ins, and links the catalog.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import SurfaceDeltas from "../components/SurfaceDeltas.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as ex } from "../classicbars.mjs";

const SPEC_BASE = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SPEC_BASE || "/flatbars/spec/";

// Sticky left-nav: ClassicBars concepts, then the sibling surfaces, then the Lab.
const groups = [
  {
    label: "ClassicBars",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "What ClassicBars is" },
      { href: "#reference", label: "Surface reference" },
      { href: "#expressions", label: "Expressions & escaping" },
      { href: "#paths", label: "Paths" },
      { href: "#identifiers", label: "Identifiers & names" },
      { href: "#conditionals", label: "if / else / unless" },
      { href: "#each", label: "each" },
      { href: "#with", label: "with" },
      { href: "#subexpressions", label: "Subexpressions & lookup" },
      { href: "#helpers", label: "Custom helpers" },
      { href: "#precompute", label: "Lambdas → values" },
      { href: "#partials", label: "Partials" },
      { href: "#comments", label: "Comments" },
      { href: "#together", label: "Putting it together" },
      { href: "#conventions", label: "Style & conventions" },
      { href: "#compile", label: "Compiles to JS" },
      { href: "#divergences", label: "Where it diverges" },
      { href: "#next", label: "Compatibility & next" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "New here?",
    items: [{ href: "/start", label: "Getting started" }],
  },
  {
    label: "Other surfaces",
    items: [
      { href: "/minbars", label: "MinBars — Mustache" },
      { href: "/rawbars", label: "RawBars — the core" },
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars — operators" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=classicbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];

// Inline tag references — kept in a helper so Astro never parses the {{ }}.
const t = (s) => s;

// Worked example (P5): a Handlebars *value* lambda → a precalculated plain-data
// field, shown as static code — the function-on-the-context you'd write and the
// JSONata that precomputes it. No live example: with the value precomputed there
// is no function left in the data to see.
const valueLambdaCode = `// Handlebars.js — a function on the context, called as {{fullName}}
const data = {
  first: "Ada", last: "Lovelace",
  fullName() { return this.first + " " + this.last; },
};`;
const jsonataCode = `/* JSONata transform — compute the value, emit plain data */
$ ~> | $ | {
  "fullName": first & " " & last,
  "initials": $uppercase($substring(first, 0, 1) & $substring(last, 0, 1))
} |`;
---

<Reference
  title="ClassicBars — Handlebars-faithful"
  subtitle="ClassicBars is faithful to Handlebars — the industry-standard {{ }} engine — and gives you its Handlebars-style surface: {{ }} auto-escaping, dotted paths, block helpers, @data, and `as |x|`. This runnable reference walks the language the way a Handlebars author already thinks."
  groups={groups}
  spec="surface"
  dialect="classicbars"
>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>What ClassicBars is</h2>
    <p>
      <b>ClassicBars is the Handlebars-faithful member of the FlatBars family.</b> If
      you know Handlebars, you already know most of this page: <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code>
      auto-escapes, dotted paths read nested data, <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> /
      <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> are block helpers, and partials, subexpressions, and
      <code>{t("{{#*inline}}")}</code> all work as you'd expect.
    </p>
    <p>
      What's underneath is different, and it's the whole point of the project.
      ClassicBars isn't a separate engine — it's a <b>surface syntax</b> over the same
      meaning-free core that runs <a href="/minbars">MinBars (Mustache)</a> and
      <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a>. One parser, four languages. In the core there
      are <b>no variables</b>: <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> doesn't read a variable,
      it <em>applies</em> the helper <code>name</code> (see <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a>).
      That model holds the line in a few places where Handlebars is looser — gathered
      under <a href="#divergences">Where ClassicBars diverges</a>.
    </p>
    <p>The simplest template — the panes are live, so start here:</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.hello.template} data={ex.hello.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      The panes are live: edit the <b>template</b> or the <b>data</b> and the output
      re-renders as you type. <b>Open in Lab</b> carries the example over to the full
      editor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Surface reference ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="reference">
    <h2>Surface reference</h2>
    <p>The syntax and value-policy deltas that define ClassicBars, at a glance — generated from the
      same single source (<code>shared/surface-syntax.json</code>) the FlatBars Lab's Reference
      popup and the spec engine page read, so it can never drift from what they render.</p>
    <SurfaceDeltas surface="classicbars" />
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────── Expressions & escaping ───────────────────── -->
  <section id="expressions">
    <h2>Expressions &amp; escaping</h2>
    <p>
      The workhorse tag. <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code> applies <code>name</code> and
      inserts the result, <b>HTML-escaped by default</b> so data can't inject markup.
      To emit raw markup use the triple-stash <code>{t("{{{name}}}")}</code>. (Handlebars's
      <code>{t("{{&name}}")}</code> is accepted as an alias, but the triple-stash is the
      canonical ClassicBars spelling.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.escaping.template} data={ex.escaping.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── Paths ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="paths">
    <h2>Paths</h2>
    <p>
      Reach into nested objects with dots — <code>{t("{{user.address.city}}")}</code> —
      and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> is the current context.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.dotted.template} data={ex.dotted.data} />
    <h3>Missing keys</h3>
    <p>
      A path that resolves to nothing — missing or <code>null</code> — renders as the
      empty string. ClassicBars never errors on a missing key.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.missing.template} data={ex.missing.data} />
    <h3>Climbing out: <code>../</code> and <code>@root</code></h3>
    <p>
      Inside a block, <code>{t("{{../x}}")}</code> climbs to the enclosing context and
      <code>{t("{{@root.x}}")}</code> jumps to the top-level data — no matter how deep
      the nesting. Both are live in the nested <a href="#each"><code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>
      example</a> below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────── Identifiers & names ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="identifiers">
    <h2>Identifiers &amp; names</h2>
    <p>
      A bare identifier — a path segment or a helper name — may be almost any
      character. Only whitespace and this reserved set are off-limits bare:
    </p>
    <p><code>{t("! \" # % & ' ( ) * + , . / ; < = > @ [ \\ ] ^ ` { | } ~")}</code></p>
    <p>
      So letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>$</code>, <code>-</code>,
      <code>:</code> and <code>?</code> are all fine bare: kebab-case keys like
      <code>{t("{{first-name}}")}</code> and Ruby/Mustache-style predicate keys like
      <code>{t("{{active?}}")}</code> need no ceremony. The reserved characters carry
      structural meaning instead — <code>.</code> and <code>/</code> separate path
      segments, <code>@</code> prefixes data variables
      (<code>{t("{{@index}}")}</code>, <code>{t("{{@root}}")}</code>), and the rest are
      operators or grouping.
    </p>
    <h3>Anything else: <code>[segment literals]</code></h3>
    <p>
      Wrap a key in square brackets to use characters that aren't bare-legal —
      spaces, dots, or reserved punctuation. This is the escape hatch:
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Write</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>a key with a space</td><td><span class="code">{t("{{[full name]}}")}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>a bracketed segment inside a path</td><td><span class="code">{t("{{address.[street name]}}")}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>a key with reserved punctuation</td><td><span class="code">{t("{{[a+b]}}")}</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Names that look like literals are literals, not lookups:
      <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, numbers, and quoted
      <code>{t("\"strings\"")}</code> — use the bracket form
      (<code>{t("{{[true]}}")}</code>) if you really have a key named <code>true</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────── if / unless ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conditionals">
    <h2>Conditionals: <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>unless</code></h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#if x}} … {{else}} … {{/if}}")}</code> is a <b>block helper</b>: its
      head is a helper application, not a bare key. The <code>{t("{{else if …}}")}</code>
      chain works too. To test more than a bare value, nest a <a href="#subexpressions">subexpression</a>
      — <code>{t("{{#if (gte stock 10)}}")}</code> is how ClassicBars writes "stock ≥ 10"
      (the infix <code>{t(">=")}</code> belongs to <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a>).
      <code>{t("{{#unless x}}")}</code> is its explicit inverse — it renders when
      <code>x</code> is falsy. Note <code>0</code> is <b>falsy</b> in ClassicBars (the
      opposite of <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a>, where a section over <code>0</code> renders).
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.conditionals.template} data={ex.conditionals.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── each ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="each">
    <h2><code>each</code> — iteration</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#each items}}")}</code> repeats its body once per element, pushing each
      element as the context. Inside, the <b><code>@data</code> variables</b>
      <code>{t("@index")}</code>, <code>{t("@first")}</code>, and <code>{t("@last")}</code>
      describe the position.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.eachList.template} data={ex.eachList.data} />
    <h3>Over an object</h3>
    <p>Iterating an object, <code>{t("@key")}</code> is the property name and <code>{t("{{this}}")}</code> the value.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.eachObject.template} data={ex.eachObject.data} />
    <h3>The empty case</h3>
    <p><code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> has its own <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code> for an empty list — no separate inverted section.</p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.eachElse.template} data={ex.eachElse.data} />
    <p class="tip">
      Rename the loop bindings with block params: <code>{t("{{#each xs as |item i|}}")}</code>
      gives the element and index your own names — useful inside nested loops.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────────────── with ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="with">
    <h2><code>with</code> — re-root the context</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{#with obj}}")}</code> makes <code>obj</code> the context for its body,
      so a deep path is written once.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.withBlock.template} data={ex.withBlock.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────── Subexpressions & lookup ────────────────────── -->
  <section id="subexpressions">
    <h2>Subexpressions &amp; <code>lookup</code></h2>
    <p>
      Parentheses nest one helper's result into another's arguments:
      <code>{t("(lookup colours selected)")}</code>. This is how ClassicBars avoids
      "write a JS helper for everything" — you <b>compose the ~80 helpers that ship</b>.
      <code>{t("{{lookup obj key}}")}</code> itself reads a field whose name isn't known
      until render — an array index, or a key chosen by the data. Below, the looked-up
      colour is upper-cased in one expression.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.subexprLookup.template} data={ex.subexprLookup.data} />
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{log x}}")}</code> is a sibling debugging aid: it writes to the console
      and renders to the empty string.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────── Custom helpers ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="helpers">
    <h2>Custom helpers</h2>
    <p>
      When the prelude isn't enough, a host registers its own with the
      Handlebars-style <code>registerHelper(name, fn)</code> — the single most common
      Handlebars extension, and it runs <b>live right here</b>. Edit the
      <b>helpers.js</b> pane below — it registers three, one of each shape:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b><code>loud</code></b> — a plain inline helper returning a string (escaped in <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code>).</li>
      <li><b><code>link</code></b> — reads trailing <code>key=value</code> <b>hash</b> arguments (<code>url=…</code>) and returns <code>safe(…)</code> raw markup.</li>
      <li><b><code>list</code></b> — a <b>block helper</b> (<a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0020-user-block-helpers/`}>ADR-020</a>): <code>options.fn(item, &#123; blockParams &#125;)</code> renders its body with a shifted context and binds <code>as |p i|</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.helpers.template} data={ex.helpers.data} helpers={ex.helpers.helpers} />
    <div class="callout info">
      <p>
        The full block <code>options</code> surface works: <code>fn</code>/<code>inverse</code>/<code>hash</code>
        plus <code>options.fn(ctx, &#123; data, blockParams &#125;)</code> — so <code>k=v</code> hash, scoped
        <code>@vars</code>, and <code>as |…|</code> block params are all supported
        (<a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0020-user-block-helpers/`}>ADR-020</a>); only
        <code>ids</code>/<code>loc</code>/<code>lookupProperty</code> throw. <code>registerHelper</code>
        is <b>ClassicBars-only</b>: RawBars and MaxBars use a different concept and terminology
        (operations / definitions, <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0019-operation-vocabulary/`}>ADR-019</a>).
        Declaring an arity (<code>registerHelper('loud', fn, 1)</code>) gives the same diagnostics
        as a built-in. In the <b>Lab</b>, helper code runs in a Worker (no page access), not a full
        sandbox — adequate for a playground.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Raw blocks</h3>
    <p>
      A <b>raw block</b> — four staches, <code>{t("{{{{helper}}}}…{{{{/helper}}}}")}</code> —
      hands its body to the helper <b>unprocessed</b>: the inner
      <code>{t("{{bar}}")}</code> is never interpreted, it's literal text the helper
      receives via <code>options.fn()</code>. Useful when a template must emit
      mustache syntax it should <em>not</em> expand (docs, code samples). Here
      <code>raw-loud</code> upper-cases the raw body, so the verbatim
      <code>{t("{{bar}}")}</code> comes out as <code>{t("{{BAR}}")}</code> — the
      data's <code>bar</code> is never read.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.rawBlock.template} data={ex.rawBlock.data} helpers={ex.rawBlock.helpers} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────── Lambdas → precalculated values ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="precompute">
    <h2>Lambdas → precalculated values</h2>
    <p>
      A helper is <em>render-time</em> behaviour. But for a computed <em>value</em>, there's a
      simpler path that needs no helper: precompute it. Handlebars lets you put a function on the
      context, called automatically as <code>{t("{{fullName}}")}</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{valueLambdaCode}</code></pre>
    <p>
      FlatBars keeps a value <b>pure data, never a function</b>, so compute it <b>before</b> rendering
      — with a <a href="/transform">JSONata transform</a> — and pass plain data:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{jsonataCode}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Now <code>{t("{{fullName}}")}</code> is a plain interpolation — identical interpreted and
      compiled, with no function in the context.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
      Rule of thumb: a computed <em>value</em> → <b>precompute</b> it (a preprocess step);
      render-time, body-aware behaviour → a <a href="#helpers">helper</a>. Either way, no function
      rides inside the data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Partials ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="partials">
    <h2>Partials</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{> name}}")}</code> includes another template and <b>inherits the caller's
      context</b> — the natural unit of reuse. Combined with <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code>
      it templates a row each, and the name can be an <b>expression</b> resolved per row —
      <code>{t("{{> (lookup this \"kind\")}}")}</code> picks the partial from the data.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.partials.template} data={ex.partials.data} partials={ex.partials.partials} />
    <h3>Layout &amp; inline partials</h3>
    <p>
      Two Handlebars-only forms. <code>{t("{{#*inline \"name\"}}…{{/inline}}")}</code> defines
      a partial inline (scoped to the rest of the template); a block partial
      <code>{t("{{#> layout}}…{{/layout}}")}</code> hands its body to the partial, which drops
      it in with <code>{t("{{> @partial-block}}")}</code> — Handlebars-style layout reuse.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.layoutPartials.template} data={ex.layoutPartials.data} partials={ex.layoutPartials.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Comments ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="comments">
    <h2>Comments</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code> is dropped from the output. The
      <code>{t("{{!-- … --}}")}</code> form may itself contain <code>{t("}}")}</code>.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.comment.template} data={ex.comment.data} />
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────── Putting it together ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="together">
    <h2>Putting it together</h2>
    <p>
      Real templates reuse these familiar pieces at once. A plain-text shipping notice —
      interpolation, a dotted path, an <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> with its built-in
      <code>{t("{{else}}")}</code>, and a <code>{t("{{> item}}")}</code> partial:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.email.template} data={ex.email.data} partials={ex.email.partials} />
    <h3>When the output <em>is</em> HTML</h3>
    <p>
      Handlebars is an HTML engine, so the capstone is an HTML card: a
      <code>{t("{{> styles}}")}</code> partial holds the CSS once, a <code>{t("{{> card}}")}</code>
      partial is one row's markup with a <code>{t("{{#if lead}}")}</code> badge, and
      <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> iterates. (The one example that previews as HTML in the Lab.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.card.template} data={ex.card.data} partials={ex.card.partials} />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────────── Style & conventions ─────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conventions">
    <h2>Style &amp; conventions</h2>
    <p>
      None of this changes what a template <em>means</em> — it changes how readable it
      is six months later. The examples on this page follow these conventions; so does
      the FlatBars Lab.
    </p>
    <ul class="conv">
      <li><b>Tight delimiters.</b> <code>{t("{{name}}")}</code>, not
        <code>{t("{{ name }}")}</code> — block tags require it (<code>{t("{{ #each }}")}</code>
        is a lex error), so tight is uniform.</li>
      <li><b>Indent with the HTML</b> (2 or 4 spaces); block bodies indent one level past
        their opener, and close tags align with that opener.</li>
      <li><b>Block helpers get their own line.</b> A block tag alone on its line is
        <em>standalone</em> — the engine trims that whole line, so the output stays tidy.</li>
      <li><b>Comment with <code>{t("{{! … }}")}</code>, not <code>&lt;!-- … --&gt;</code></b> —
        a Handlebars comment is dropped before render; an HTML comment ships to the browser.</li>
      <li><b>Reach for <code>~</code> sparingly.</b> A tilde
        (<code>{t("{{~name~}}")}</code>) trims adjacent whitespace; format for humans first,
        add tildes only where the output needs them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────── Compiles to JS ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="compile">
    <h2>Compiles to JS</h2>
    <p>
      Like Handlebars's precompilation, ClassicBars <b>compiles a template to a JS
      module</b> — and FlatBars gates that the compiled module and the interpreter
      produce <b>byte-identical</b> output (so the pane below is the same render, just
      ahead of time). Expand <b>Compiled JS</b> on the conditionals example:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={ex.conditionals.template} data={ex.conditionals.data} compile />
  </section>

  <!-- ───────────────── Where ClassicBars diverges ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="divergences">
    <h2>Where ClassicBars diverges from Handlebars</h2>
    <p>
      Most of Handlebars works unchanged. A few things differ <b>by design</b> — each a
      consequence of the meaning-free core, each with a one-line replacement. The
      normative list is <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/classicbars-compat/`}>classicbars-compat.adoc</a>;
      this table is the short version.
    </p>
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Handlebars</th><th>ClassicBars</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="code">else</span> as a keyword</td>
          <td><span class="code">else</span> is a <b>separator</b>; <span class="code">elif</span> is a real clause</td>
          <td>The core has no keywords (not even <span class="code">else</span>); a block splits on a name-agnostic separator.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="code">true</span> / <span class="code">false</span> / <span class="code">null</span> literals</td>
          <td>the same spellings — but they're <b>helpers</b></td>
          <td>The core's only literals are strings and numbers; <span class="code">true</span>/<span class="code">false</span>/<span class="code">null</span> aren't keywords but nullary helpers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mustache set delimiters <span class="code">{t("{{=<% %>=}}")}</span></td>
          <td>fixed <span class="code">{t("{{ }}")}</span> / <span class="code">{t("{{{ }}}")}</span></td>
          <td>A fixed grammar keeps the parser meaning-free and the tooling simple. (A Mustache feature; Handlebars fixes the delimiters too.)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Data/lambdas as functions in the context</td>
          <td>precalculate the value (preprocess), or a <a href="#helpers">helper</a> for body-aware sections</td>
          <td>A value is pure data, never a function. A value-producing lambda is precalculated before rendering (any host computation; the Lab uses JSONata); a section lambda that rewrites its body is a helper. No host code runs from template data.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      Coming from Mustache or Handlebars? An <b>implicit section</b> just works:
      <code>{t("{{#person}}…{{/person}}")}</code> renders once when <code>person</code> is
      truthy and <code>{t("{{#tags}}…{{/tags}}")}</code> iterates a list — ClassicBars looks an
      unregistered block head up as data and dispatches it like Handlebars'
      <code>blockHelperMissing</code> (array&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>each</code>,
      truthy&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>with</code>, empty&nbsp;⇒&nbsp;<code>{t("{{else}}")}</code>).
      Writing <code>{t("{{#if}}")}</code> / <code>{t("{{#each}}")}</code> explicitly is still
      the clearer style. ClassicBars matches Handlebars on <b>98%</b> of a differential
      conformance corpus (rendered through the real Handlebars engine); the one corpus miss is a
      raw-block corner (user-defined block helpers now pass — <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}adr/adr-0020-user-block-helpers/`}>ADR-020</a>).
      In-data lambdas aren't a gap: a value is pure data, so value-producing lambdas are precalculated
      before rendering and section lambdas are helpers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────── Compatibility & next ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="next">
    <h2>Compatibility &amp; where to go next</h2>
    <p>
      The examples above are the Handlebars guide's own building blocks — expressions,
      paths, the built-in helpers, subexpressions, partials, custom helpers — running
      <b>unchanged on ClassicBars</b>. The complete concept-by-concept mapping (with the
      core each one desugars to) is the normative
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}appendix/handlebars/`}>Handlebars → FlatBars appendix</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>The other ~70 helpers</h3>
    <p>
      This page tours the six built-ins; the rest of the prelude — string, array, math,
      and comparison helpers like <code>uppercase</code>, <code>join</code>,
      <code>add</code>, <code>sortBy</code>, <code>eq</code> — lives in the generated
      <a href={`${SPEC_BASE}engine/prelude/`}>helper catalog</a>, which is the single source
      of truth (it can't drift from the engine).
    </p>
    <h3>Up and down the ladder</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a> is what ClassicBars desugars <em>to</em> — the
      meaning-free core. <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> adds infix operators and pipes
      on top — so <code>{t("{{#if (gte score 50)}}")}</code> here becomes
      <code>{t("{{#if score >= 50}}")}</code> there. Same engine, same compiler. Or jump
      straight into the <a href="/lab/index.html?engine=classicbars">Lab</a> and start typing.
    </p>
  </section>
